  "text": "To find the largest integer \\( n \\) such that there are at least 5 primes whose squares end in the same \\( n \\) digits, we need to consider the properties of prime numbers and their squares. Specifically, we need to look at the last digits of the squares of prime numbers.\n\nPrime numbers greater than 5 end in one of the digits 1, 3, 7, or 9. When squared, the last digits of these primes will be:\n- \\(1^2 = 1\\)\n- \\(3^2 = 9\\)\n- \\(7^2 = 49\\), so the last digit is 9\n- \\(9^2 = 81\\), so the last digit is 1\n\nThis means that the last digits of the squares of prime numbers greater than 5 are either 1 or 9. Therefore, the last digits of the squares of prime numbers are limited to the digits 1 and 9.\n\nNow, we need to check if there are at least 5 primes whose squares end in the same last digit. Since the only possible last digits for the squares of prime numbers are 1 and 9, the only way to have at least 5 primes ending in the same last digit is if we consider the last digits of the squares of prime numbers in a sequence where the last digit of the squares repeats.\n\nFor example, if we consider the sequence of prime numbers ending in 1 (like 11, 31, 41, 61, 71), their squares will end in 1. Similarly, if we consider the sequence of prime numbers ending in 9 (like 19, 29, 59, 79, 89), their squares will also end in 1.\n\nThus, we can see that there are at least 5 prime numbers whose squares end in the same last digit (1 in this case). The last digit of these squares is 1.\n\nTherefore, the largest integer \\( n \\) such that there are at least 5 primes whose squares end in the same \\( n \\) digits is \\( n = 1 \\).\n\nThe final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]",
